<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rock of Dunamase</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rock of Dunamase is a limestone outcrop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-laois">County Laois</a>, Ireland, crowned by the ruins of a castle. A fortress there was plundered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> in 845 and later held by the kings of Leinster. After the Anglo-Norman invasion it passed by marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a> and then to the Marshal family, who built the stone castle; it was in ruins by the 17th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rock-of-dunamase.jpg" alt="Rock of Dunamase" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kent Clemmons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_of_Dunamase.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dunamase, in Irish Dún Másc or the fort of Másc, rises 46 metres above the surrounding plain near the N80 road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portlaoise">Portlaoise</a> and Stradbally, with a view across to the Slieve Bloom Mountains. It was one of the landmarks shown on Ptolemy's map of the year 140. Excavations in the 1990s showed that the rock was first settled in the 9th century, when a hill fort was built on it, and found no clear evidence of occupation in the 10th and 11th centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> attack of 845 the abbot of Terryglass, Aed son of Dub dá Chrích, was killed at Dunamase. After the Normans arrived it became the most important Hiberno-Norman fortification in Laois. Dermot MacMurrough, King of Leinster, brought the kidnapped wife of O'Rourke, King of Breifne, there; the O'Rourkes and O'Connors drove him out and he fled Ireland. In 1170 he gave Dunamase and his daughter Aoife in marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>, as part of a bargain for help in regaining his lands.</p><p>Through Isabel, the daughter and heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a> and Aoife, the castle passed to the Marshal family. The five sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-marshal-1st-earl-of-pembroke">William Marshal</a>, Regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the minority of Henry III, each died without issue, and in 1247 the Marshal lands were divided among his five daughters. Dunamase fell to Eva Marshal and then to her daughter Maud, wife of Roger Mortimer. It stayed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-mortimer">Mortimer family</a> until 1330, when another Roger Mortimer was executed for treason, and it seems to have become a ruinous shell by 1350.</p><p>From the 1400s to the 16th century the rock lay in the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omore">O'More</a> family, whose resistance to the English is remembered in the county's nickname, the O'Moore County. Rory O'More, Lord of Laois, held the castle. The O'Mores later moved to land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a> granted in 1574 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I of England</a>. The castle played no part in the Cromwellian wars but was slighted in 1650 so that it could not be used. In the later 18th century Sir John Parnell began a banqueting hall within the ruins, using medieval details taken from other sites nearby.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dunamase” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Rock of Dunamase</b> was a royal stronghold before the Anglo-Normans took it in the late 12th century and built a castle there.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-loigis">Kingdom of Loigis</a></li></ul></section>
